-- Version 18 → 19: what each user has already read.
--
-- Two new tables and nothing else — the same shape as 17.sqm, and for the same
-- reason: LNL-64's whole storage is two high-water marks, and there is nowhere
-- else in the schema for "how far have I got" to be recorded.
--
-- The dual-path story is 1.sqm's and 2.sqm's: a fresh volume runs `CREATE TABLE`
-- out of Reads.sq and gets both of these for free; a volume already at version
-- 18 runs this instead. MigrationTest migrates every earlier snapshot forward
-- and compares PRAGMA output against a fresh `Schema.create`, so drift between
-- the two files fails the build rather than the volume.
--
-- ── Order does not matter, as in 17.sqm ─────────────────────────────────────
--
-- Both point at things that already exist — `users`, `conversations`, `forums` —
-- and neither points at the other.
--
-- ── What existing rows get: nothing, and the alternative is worse ───────────
--
-- Neither table is backfilled, so on the morning this deploys **everything is
-- unread**: every conversation shows its whole history as new, and the Discussion
-- tab wears a dot.
--
-- That is deliberate, and the two alternatives were both written out. Marking
-- everything read would be the server claiming, on everybody's behalf, that they
-- have seen messages they may well not have — and the one thing a read state must
-- not do is lie in that direction, because an unread badge that was never true is
-- how people stop trusting the badge. Marking everything from *now* would need a
-- row per user per conversation and per forum at migration time, which is the
-- fan-out this design exists to avoid, written once at the worst possible moment.
--
-- A day of stale-looking badges, cleared by opening the things, is the cheapest
-- honest answer. Worth stating rather than being discovered by whoever deploys it.
--
-- The statements below reproduce Reads.sq's two CREATE TABLE blocks, minus the
-- prose. See that file for what each constraint is for — and in particular for
-- why `last_read_message_id` has no foreign key, which is the one thing here that
-- would look like an omission.

CREATE TABLE conversation_reads (
    user_id INTEGER NOT NULL,
    conversation_id INTEGER NOT NULL,
    last_read_message_id INTEGER NOT NULL,
    PRIMARY KEY (user_id, conversation_id),
    FOREIGN KEY (user_id) REFERENCES users(id) ON DELETE CASCADE,
    FOREIGN KEY (conversation_id) REFERENCES conversations(id) ON DELETE CASCADE
);

CREATE TABLE forum_reads (
    user_id INTEGER NOT NULL,
    forum_id INTEGER NOT NULL,
    last_read_at INTEGER NOT NULL,
    PRIMARY KEY (user_id, forum_id),
    FOREIGN KEY (user_id) REFERENCES users(id) ON DELETE CASCADE,
    FOREIGN KEY (forum_id) REFERENCES forums(id) ON DELETE CASCADE
);
